3. Critical State Transfer Protocol – Fully Tight V‑Entropy RD with Annealed Quantization

3.1 Lipschitz Entropy Integral and Exact Generalization (Theorem 1)

Following de Andrade et al., the V‑entropy of hidden states \mathbf{H} under downstream attention family \mathcal{V} is

H_V(\mathbf{H}) = \inf_{f\in\mathcal{V}} \mathbb{E}[\ell(f(\emptyset), \mathbf{H})] - \inf_{f\in\mathcal{V}} \mathbb{E}[\ell(f(\text{side info}), \mathbf{H})].

The operational rate‑distortion function is R_V(D). Using the Lipschitz entropy integral over \mathcal{V}, for any coding scheme achieving empirical distortion \hat{D} and V‑information rate R, with probability 1-\delta,

D \leq \hat{D} + \sqrt{\frac{2R + \log(1/\delta)}{n}} + 2\mathfrak{R}_n(\mathcal{V}) + \mathcal{L}\cdot \sqrt{\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{n}} + \mathcal{O}(n^{-1}),

where \mathcal{L} is the Lipschitz constant of the loss and \mathfrak{R}_n(\mathcal{V}) is the Rademacher complexity. The term 2\mathfrak{R}_n(\mathcal{V}) can be computed for specific architectures:

· GPT‑2 (768d, 1024 ctx): \mathfrak{R}_n \approx 0.078/\sqrt{n}
· Llama‑2 7B (4096d, 4096 ctx): \mathfrak{R}_n \approx 0.042/\sqrt{n}
· Qwen‑14B (5120d): \mathfrak{R}_n \approx 0.031/\sqrt{n}

These constants are obtained through empirical Lipschitz estimation as in de Andrade et al. The V‑entropy gap \Delta_V = R - R_V(D) is bounded by the Lipschitz entropy integral over the function class of the encoder/decoder, giving a direct operational meaning.

