Appendix A: Mathematical Background

This appendix provides the foundational concepts used throughout the AETHERFLOW theoretical framework.

A.1 V‑Entropy and V‑Information

Let \mathcal{V} be a family of functions, and let X, Y be random variables. The V‑information from X to Y is defined as:

I_\mathcal{V}(X \to Y) = \inf_{f \in \mathcal{V}} \mathbb{E}\big[ \ell(f(\emptyset), Y) \big] - \mathbb{E}\big[ \ell(f(X), Y) \big],

where \ell is a loss function (typically log loss). The V‑entropy of Y is H_\mathcal{V}(Y) = \inf_{f \in \mathcal{V}} \mathbb{E}[\ell(f(\emptyset), Y)].
The operational rate‑distortion function under V‑information constraint, when transmitting a source H to be used by a downstream function class \mathcal{V}, is:

R_V(D) = \inf_{p_{\hat{H}|H}} I_\mathcal{V}(H \to \hat{H}) \quad \text{subject to} \quad \mathbb{E}[d(H, \hat{H})] \leq D.

This formulation, due to de Andrade et al. (2026), replaces classical mutual information with V‑information, yielding tighter bounds when the reconstruction is not used by an ideal observer but by a specific computational model.

A.2 Rademacher Complexity and Generalization

For a function class \mathcal{F} on n i.i.d. samples, the empirical Rademacher complexity is

\hat{\mathfrak{R}}_n(\mathcal{F}) = \mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{\sigma}} \left[ \sup_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i f(z_i) \right],

where \sigma_i are independent Rademacher variables. Bounds on the supremum of empirical processes involve the Rademacher complexity, often through the Lipschitz entropy integral.

A.3 Power‑Law Distributions and Self‑Organized Criticality

A random variable S follows a power‑law tail if \mathbb{P}(S > s) \propto s^{-\alpha} for large s, with \alpha \in (2,3) typical for token criticality. Self‑organized criticality (SoC) describes systems that maintain a critical state with power‑law distributed avalanches without fine‑tuning. The tail exponent \alpha can drift slowly, modeled as an Ornstein‑Uhlenbeck process:

d\alpha_t = -\kappa(\alpha_t - \bar{\alpha}) dt + \sigma dW_t.

A.4 Conformal Prediction and Adaptive Risk Control

Split conformal prediction provides distribution‑free prediction sets with coverage guarantees. For a nonconformity score R(x_i, y_i), a calibration set yields a threshold \hat{q} such that for a new sample,  \mathbb{P}(Y_{n+1} \in C(X_{n+1})) \geq 1-\alpha .
ORCA (Online Risk Control with Adaptive thresholds) extends this to sequential settings, allowing online adjustment of thresholds to control a risk measure like false detection rate over time.

A.5 Random Matrix Theory for Attention

The attention weight matrix A (with softmax) after token removal can be modeled as a deformed Wigner matrix. Its eigenvalue distribution in the large n limit follows the Marchenko‑Pastur law with parameter \rho = d_{\text{head}} / n_{\text{seq}}. The largest eigenvalue fluctuations follow the Tracy‑Widom distribution with n^{-2/3} scaling. Spectral collapse occurs when the largest eigenvalue merges with the bulk, signaling loss of information propagation.

